<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Othman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1351">1351</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Othman was the Marinid sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> from 1331 to 1348 and the dynasty's most ambitious ruler. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> in 1337 but was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Portugal at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rio-salado">Battle of Río Salado</a> in 1340. In 1347 he occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, briefly uniting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, but Arab tribes defeated him near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, and his son Abu Inan took his throne; he died in 1351.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-al-hasan-ali-ibn-othman.jpg" alt="Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Othman" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alif · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abu_al-Hasan_ben_Uthman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu al-Hasan was the son of the Marinid ruler Abu Sa'id Uthman II, whom he succeeded in 1331. According to the chronicler al-Baydhaq his mother was a woman of Fes called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a>, and his dark complexion earned him the name of the Black Sultan. He married Fatima, daughter of the Hafsid ruler Abu Yahya Abu Bakr II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, sealing an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayyanid-dynasty">Zayyanid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1333, answering an appeal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iv-of-granada">Muhammad IV of Granada</a>, he sent an army of 7,000 under his son Abd al-Malik across the strait to besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> since 1309. Its starving defenders, reduced to eating leather, surrendered on 17 June 1333. When Ibn Tashufin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsids</a>, Abu al-Hasan invaded in early 1335 and besieged Tlemcen for two years, turning his camp into a city beside it; Ibn Tashufin died in the final assault, and the conquest gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> control of the trans-Saharan trade.</p><p>In 1339 Yusuf I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> called on him against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. His fleet destroyed a Castilian squadron off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> on 5 April 1340, and in September his army joined Yusuf's to besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a>. A Portuguese and Genoese fleet cut his supply line, and Afonso IV of Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xi-of-castile">Alfonso XI of Castile</a> then defeated him; he made no further campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, and Alfonso XI took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> in March 1344. After the Hafsid sultan Abu Bakr died in 1346, Abu al-Hasan's army swept through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, and contemporaries compared his realm with that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His attempt to impose his authority on the Arab tribes led to their revolt. After the defeat of April 1348, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-inan-faris">Abu Inan Faris</a> declared himself sultan in Fes, his fleet was wrecked by a storm off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bougie</a>, and the Zayyanid princes beat him near the Chelif River. Defeated again by Abu Inan on the Oum er-Rebia in May 1350, he took refuge among the Hintata tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a> and abdicated in late 1350 or early 1351. He died there in May 1351, and Abu Inan had his body carried to the Marinid necropolis at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chellah">Chellah</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Uthman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fez, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. Died in High Atlas, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1341" aria-label="Fez on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4423 930 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Othman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4873 1200h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4873 1200h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1341">What was here in 1341? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
